#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903d2545-fa90-55e5-ba2b-e6308e7de79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177551} {:answer/id #uuid "96d1135a-b518-548a-921d-6220cbeb1e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177552} {:answer/id #uuid "28559a61-4ad2-5526-9041-6d8c1cb1e3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177553} {:answer/id #uuid "5b714c4a-fd28-5f3a-b3be-dd739cf8ea10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177554} {:answer/id #uuid "7a559f7d-bc37-53f7-98ed-e27af3cd6813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96d1135a-b518-548a-921d-6220cbeb1e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177552} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c323da1-73db-5bab-8d9c-521c90f955f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "167f2fd7-c35e-5f43-aff5-1c9b26e281cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-124114" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-y+1,\\,v=by-x+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot x+1\\cdot (y-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+1 \\\\ y-x-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-y+1,\\,v=1y-x+(-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+(-1)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4a4ae6-65bc-517e-84e7-7361ef4f02c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177557} {:answer/id #uuid "22878e9c-f021-5c71-96a5-f367c7a123be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177558} {:answer/id #uuid "25fca980-9de6-5124-8969-7cd05d54734a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177559} {:answer/id #uuid "35675bff-bf9d-503c-a46b-5fa26918b288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177560} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8ffe6-5223-58e7-93c8-9f6d842d167e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35675bff-bf9d-503c-a46b-5fa26918b288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177560} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9e34c89-5701-5227-9f01-703dd6a84fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24203ad0-c666-584c-ace2-35e7e7f927ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124117" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+1+(-8)=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a409b5-603b-59ba-a293-2c7dc9d925c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354337} {:answer/id #uuid "e62df2d2-711b-5010-beb3-70ed3ba42ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354338} {:answer/id #uuid "b409bffb-45af-5b54-af50-89247a1a0b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354339} {:answer/id #uuid "504ed4e4-7836-5486-993c-4635ecb0294e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354340} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f77a26-1543-5055-8f7c-c06ae6f3f69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a409b5-603b-59ba-a293-2c7dc9d925c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354337} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27332fa-356d-5922-87ea-b419456c01ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "feab6d6e-33b5-5eb2-bb1f-ab10bdc595ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-124235" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ a & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+\\frac{3}{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2})=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2795aa-bf46-557e-85ff-dc8d84919010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354344} {:answer/id #uuid "ad75057a-afa1-5b92-afd8-466159757648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354345} {:answer/id #uuid "06c77987-242f-5694-9817-70165eecc4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354346} {:answer/id #uuid "bdea084c-4bc0-59c9-9d68-bd11ad1f47fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354347} {:answer/id #uuid "68acfe75-6c82-558d-bf10-5b8068628003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a2795aa-bf46-557e-85ff-dc8d84919010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354344} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5d03d6-13c2-5935-a0a5-45865204f063" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e3a5a3c5-1708-5cd7-9807-5f4074faf005" :question/math-academy-id "q-124315" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5u-9v-2,\\,y=4u-8v+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-\\frac{9}{4}y+b,\\,v=cx-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-9v-2 \\\\ 4u-8v+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-9v \\\\ 4u-8v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-8\\cdot 5-(-9)\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-4}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+2 \\\\ y-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x+2)-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (y-4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+2)+(-\\frac{5}{4})\\cdot (y-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13 \\\\ x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13,\\,v=1x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+13+1=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f34976aa-fc77-51c8-a4fd-b0f855832836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354351} {:answer/id #uuid "960fac8e-9206-5f5d-adf9-9eff3033f8d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354352} {:answer/id #uuid "452fc7ad-66ac-5f31-89c3-d118473678ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354353} {:answer/id #uuid "265a75c5-7dbc-5c02-9203-807374b33342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354354} {:answer/id #uuid "b3740718-3df4-5ef9-85cf-58607b33c6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "960fac8e-9206-5f5d-adf9-9eff3033f8d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354352} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a7d3d4a-7ea2-532d-8f87-fd8bd24a8b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f811f6dd-e70f-56f6-bb39-b296a41aff91" :question/math-academy-id "q-162196" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066229/assets/576d60f179b56159504efc59178ada3db798046d01a7dbc92986cd1162f770ea.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u+2v+1,\\,y=2u+v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v+1 \\\\ 2u+v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram."}]}
